					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/roundwoodsmonster" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5041695); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5041695&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606101297" alt="roundwoodsmonster" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5041695)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/roundwoodsmonster" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5041695); return false;" >roundwoodsmonster</a></span> <span class="odate time_1548633783 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Jan 2019 00:03</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4132869">
									

<p>I like the idea behind this. But I do have some concerns regarding it:</p>
<ul>
<li>A minimum of 15,536 people played this game before the Foundation discovered it. And it's clear that the game was online for multiple months (otherwise, there would be no monthly average). It's fair to assume that many thousands of messages were left before the Foundation took the game offline. That's&#8230; that's gotta be a lot of random events - all of which reference pop culture in some way - occurring all over the world. And considering how many famous films and TV shows are set in heavily populated areas (NYC, for example)&#8230; well, the idea that people wouldn't notice that something was up is a little far-fetched. I'd suggest making it harder for people to leave anomalous messages. Maybe the message only sets off a real-world event if the player gets a particularly high score, or only when they reach the top of an especially challenging building.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>What's stopping the players from typing a completely original phrase in as their message? Why are they seemingly limited to pop culture references?</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>That second-to-last test, which&#8230; I, uh. I think I'm too young to accurately judge the tastefulness of that one.</li>
</ul>
<p>The concept here is definitely interesting, and I'd love to see it succeed. But I do think it needs some revising in order to be successful. For now, I'm not casting a vote either way.</p>

							</div>

								
					
